    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Nederland sits in Mid-County Jefferson County, between Port Arthur and Beaumont and alongside Port Neches and Groves. It is a residential community with established neighborhoods, local businesses, and close ties to the refining and petrochemical industry that employs many Southeast Texas families. If your Nederland property has been damaged, a public adjuster in Nederland can help you understand your policies, document the loss, and present the claim to your insurer. Port Arthur Public Adjuster serves Nederland as part of our Jefferson County service area.</p>

<h2>Nederland and the Harvey rainfall record</h2>
<p>Nederland has a place in U.S. weather history. According to the <a href="https://www.weather.gov/lch/2017Harvey" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Weather Service in Lake Charles</a>, the highest storm total rainfall reported from Hurricane Harvey was <strong>60.58 inches near Nederland</strong>, a U.S. tropical cyclone rainfall record at the time. Across Jefferson County, widespread storm totals topped 40 inches between August 24 and 31, 2017, and more than 64,000 homes flooded countywide.</p>
<p>Two years later, Tropical Storm Imelda brought extreme rainfall and widespread flash flooding across Southeast Texas in September 2019. Nederland property owners also lived through Hurricane Rita's wind damage in 2005, Hurricane Ike's regional wind damage in 2008, and the damaging winds Hurricane Laura brought to Jefferson County in 2020. Use the <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a> to see reports near Nederland for a specific date.</p>

<h2>What that means for your insurance</h2>
<h3>Rain flooding is still flood</h3>
<p>Many Nederland homes that flooded in Harvey and Imelda were not near a bayou or the coast. That surprises people, but most insurance policies treat surface water from extreme rainfall as flood. Standard homeowners, commercial, and TWIA policies generally exclude flood, so that damage is normally covered only by an NFIP or private flood policy. NFIP policies typically have a 30-day waiting period, so coverage needs to be in place well before a storm. See <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood damage claims</a>.</p>

<h3>Wind and wind-driven rain</h3>
<p>Water that enters through a roof, window, or wall opening created by wind is a different matter and may be covered under the wind portion of your coverage, whether that is a TWIA policy or a private homeowners policy. When both wind and flood damage are present, careful documentation of each is important: roof and exterior damage, ceiling stains and wet insulation from above, and high-water lines from below. Our article on <a href="/wind-vs-flood-damage-port-arthur-insurance/">wind vs. flood damage</a> explains how insurers draw that line.</p>

<h3>TWIA and windstorm inspections</h3>
<p>Nederland is in the TWIA catastrophe area. If wind and hail are excluded from your homeowners policy, your TWIA policy is where a hurricane roof claim goes. TWIA claims must generally be reported within one year of the date of loss, and repairs, including re-roofs and roof repairs, generally need TDI windstorm inspection and a WPI-8 certificate. Read the <a href="/twia-windstorm-claim-guide-port-arthur/">TWIA windstorm claim guide</a>.</p>

<h2>Nederland homes and buildings</h2>
<p>Nederland's housing is largely single-family homes, many of them slab-on-grade with composition shingle roofs and brick veneer or vinyl or fiber-cement siding, with some older pier-and-beam houses. Commercial properties along the main roads include retail centers, restaurants, offices, and service businesses, often with low-slope or metal roofs. Common claim issues we review include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Shingle damage from hurricane and thunderstorm winds that is approved only as a partial repair when matching or code issues may require more</li>
<li>Interior water damage from roof leaks that is attributed to "wear and tear"</li>
<li>Flood claims where drywall, insulation, and flooring removal heights are underestimated</li>
<li>Mold that develops after a water loss in Southeast Texas humidity</li>
<li>Burst pipes from hard freezes like February 2021</li>
</ul>
<p>See <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a> and <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> for more detail.</p>

